 ("ul", ["1 < √2 < 2, and closer bounds: 1.4 < √2 < 1.5, 1.41 < √2 < 1.42, **1.414 < √2 < 1.415**; √2 = 1.41421356…", "√2 cannot be a **terminating decimal**: a terminating decimal ending in a non-zero digit has a square ending in a non-zero digit, so its square can never be exactly 2.000…", "√2 cannot be written as a fraction m/n of counting numbers. If 2n² = m², the prime 2 would appear an odd number of times on the left and an even number of times on the right. (Proof by Euclid, c. 300 BCE.)", "So √2 has a **non-terminating** decimal expansion."]),
 ("h2", "Combining Two Different Squares"),
 ("p", "Baudhāyana (Verse 1.12): *the square on the diagonal produces the sum of the squares on the two sides.* To combine two squares of sides a and b, make a right-angled triangle whose perpendicular sides are a and b. The square on its hypotenuse has area equal to a² + b²."),
 ("note", "FORMULA", "**Baudhāyana–Pythagoras Theorem:** in a right-angled triangle with perpendicular sides **a** and **b** and hypotenuse **c**, **a² + b² = c²**."),
 ("p", "Proof idea: four congruent right triangles with sides a, b, c are placed around a square so that the four-sided figure formed is a square of side c. Its area equals the sum of the areas of the two original squares."),
