 ("note", "DEFINITION", "A **Baudhāyana (Pythagorean) triple** (a, b, c) is a set of positive integers with **a² + b² = c²**. A triple with no common factor greater than 1 is **primitive**."),
 ("ul", ["Baudhāyana listed (3, 4, 5), (5, 12, 13), (8, 15, 17), (7, 24, 25), (12, 35, 37) and (15, 36, 39).", "All triples with numbers up to 20: (3, 4, 5), (6, 8, 10), (9, 12, 15), (12, 16, 20), (5, 12, 13), (8, 15, 17). The primitive ones are (3, 4, 5), (5, 12, 13) and (8, 15, 17).", "**Scaling rule:** if (a, b, c) is a triple, so is (ka, kb, kc) for every positive integer k, because (ka)² + (kb)² = k²(a² + b²) = k²c² = (kc)². Hence there are **infinitely many** triples.", "Every triple is a primitive triple or a scaled version of one."]),
